  "query_preview": "..\n(Monday night through Saturday)\nIssued at 325 AM CDT Sun Aug 30 2026\n\nThe axis of an upper ridge meanders over the eastern CONUS through \nmidweek, then retrogrades toward the Plains by the end of the week. \nThis leaves our region mainly on the western side of the ridge, \nallowing for persistent hot and mostly dry conditions. Each day of \nthe forecast period shall look very similar to the last below this \nrelatively stagnant weather pattern. Afternoon highs are forecast to \nrange within the 90s to low triple digits. Highs beyond the century \nmark will tend to be found within the northeastern Permian Basin \nand areas near and along the river valleys. Overnight lows will cool \nto the 60s in the higher terrain, with low to mid 70s on tap for \neveryone else. Although September 1st is the start of climatological \nFall, summer-like heat shall remain firmly in place. Rain chances \nacross the region generally look slim to none through the Long Term \nperiod. However, a few isolated showers and storms will be possible \nover the higher terrain west of the Pecos River most afternoons. \nThese driven by upslope flow and disturbances riding along the edge \nof the ridge. Otherwise, dry conditions to hold for most.",
